# The offset base x: 6 digits here (as GNU), 7 for macOS; the standard leaves the format of the offset open.
-A x text
-A x bytes256
-A d -A x text
# Empty input: the offset after the last byte is written (0000000), macOS writes nothing.
empty
in:empty
in:empty -A d
-A x empty
-j 0 empty
-N 5 empty
-N 5 -A d empty
-A n empty
in:empty -A n
# -t a: only the seven low bits count and each is named; macOS writes the hexadecimal value for a byte over 127.
-t a bytes256
# Items of 8 bytes (d8 o8 x8 dL): macOS does not read them as 8 bytes, and it does not extend the last one with
# null bytes as the standard asks.
-t x8 negative
-t o8 -N 3 bytes256
-t d8 -N 17 negative
-t dL negative
-t o8 negative
-t d8 negative
-t x8 -N 9 bytes256
-t x8 -N 40 zeros
-t x1 -t x2 -t x4 -t x8 short
# Floating point: macOS writes a fixed number of digits (1.000000e+00), here the fewest that read back as the same number
# (GNU does the same). Lines with several types that include f differ in that line alone.
-t f8 float64
-t fD float64
-t f float64
-t f4 float64
-t f4 float32
-t fF float32
-t f8 float32
-t f4 -t f8 float64
-t f8 -N 3 float64
-t f4 -N 5 float32
-t acdfoux bytes256
-t xuofdca bytes256
-t a -t c -t d -t f -t o -t u -t x bytes256
-t x -t u -t o -t f -t d -t c -t a bytes256
# macOS writes the numbers of a field so close that no blank is left between them; the standard has them separated by blanks.
-t dC negative
-t d1x negative
-t d1 bytes256
# -j: the standard has an error if the input is shorter than skip; macOS writes the offset. A leading 0 is octal (08 is an
# error), 0x needs a digit.
-j 2k -N 20 -A d big
-j 08 text
-j 100 text
-j 257 -t x1 bytes256
-j 200 text dup
-j 0x text
# -N: 0, a number with 0x and a number in octal: macOS refuses 0 and 0x10 and reads 010 as decimal, 08 as 8.
-N 0 text
-N 0 -A d text
-N 010 text
-N 08 text
-N 0x10 text
# The file operand -: standard input (the standard lets the implementation take it so); macOS looks for a file named -.
-
in:text text -
in:text - text
in:text -
in:text -A d - -
in:text -N 5 -
# A directory among the files: both say it cannot be read; the status is 1 here and 0 for macOS.
dir
text dir
-A d dir text
# The offset operand: the standard has it octal, with . decimal and b in blocks of 512; ours writes the offset in the base
# of -A (octal), macOS in the base of the operand, and macOS takes it also with -A -j -N -t -v and after other operands.
big 1.b
big +1.b
big +2.b
text +010.
text 3.
text +3.
in:text +3
in:text +3.
text +1 +2
-A d text +3
-t x1 text +3
-j 1 text +3
-v text +3
-N 5 text +3
text 1 2
-c big 3.b
# A character cut by the end of an output line: shown at its first byte with ** on the next line (the standard); macOS writes octal.
-j 1 -t c utf8
-A d -t c '😀 file.txt' utf8
